>
  <header>
    <div class="header-left">
      <h1>Paleo Diet</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Lean meats, fish, fruits, and vegetables — modeled on pre-agricultural eating.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="ghost-btn" href="/diets/">← Back to Diets</a>
  </header>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">What Is the Paleo Diet?</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        The paleo diet is an eating pattern modeled on foods presumed available before the rise of agriculture:
        lean meats, fish, eggs, vegetables, fruit, nuts, and seeds. It excludes grains, legumes, dairy, refined
        sugar, and processed foods, on the reasoning that these farming-era foods are evolutionarily recent
        relative to hunter-gatherer eating. In practice, it works less by counting anything and more by
        restricting entire food categories rather than calories or macronutrients.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">How It Works</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Paleo is modeled on a presumed hunter-gatherer eating pattern: lean meats, fish, fruits, vegetables,
        nuts, and seeds, while excluding grains, legumes, dairy, refined sugar, and processed foods. The
        rationale is that human genetics and physiology haven't had much time to adapt to farming-era foods
        (grains, legumes, dairy), which are evolutionarily recent relative to hunter-gatherer eating.
        <br /><br />
        Worth knowing: this "one ancestral diet" premise has been directly challenged in the scientific
        literature. Lieberman DE, et al., "Comparing measured dietary variation within and between tropical
        hunter-gatherer groups to the Paleo Diet," <em>American Journal of Clinical Nutrition</em>, 2023,
        analyzed 11 tropical forager groups and found their macronutrient ratios varied far more widely than
        the range Paleo assumes — concluding the ethnographic record doesn't support the idea of one
        circumscribed ancestral macronutrient pattern. In plain terms: there wasn't a single "paleo diet" that
        actual hunter-gatherers ate.
        <br /><br />
        <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37343704/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the critique →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">The Research</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        The evidence base here is real but noticeably thinner and shorter-term than Mediterranean or DASH —
        a handful of small trials running weeks to months, not decades of cohort data.
      </p>
      <strong>Trial:</strong> Lindeberg S, Jönsson T, Granfeldt Y, et al. "A Palaeolithic diet improves glucose
      tolerance more than a Mediterranean-like diet in individuals with ischaemic heart disease," <em>Diabetologia</em>, 2007. A 12-week RCT of 29 people with ischemic heart disease and impaired glucose
      tolerance found the paleo group's glucose tolerance improved substantially more than the
      Mediterranean-like diet group's, independent of the change in waist circumference.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17583796/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the trial →</a>
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Additional research:</strong>
      <br /><br />
      Ghaedi E, Mohammadi M, Mohammadi H, et al. "Effects of a Paleolithic Diet on Cardiovascular Disease Risk
      Factors: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials," <em>Advances in
      Nutrition</em>, 2019. Pooling 8 RCTs, paleo diets modestly improved weight, waist circumference, blood
      pressure, and lipid markers versus comparison diets — but the authors themselves caution the results
      were sensitive to which studies were included, and call the evidence "not conclusive." A published
      comment on this same meta-analysis flagged calculation errors in it, so treat its exact effect sizes with
      caution even while the general direction (modest short-term improvement) is plausible.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31041449/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the meta-analysis →</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Food Examples</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        <strong>Emphasized:</strong> grass-fed beef, chicken, wild-caught salmon, eggs, almonds, walnuts,
        pumpkin seeds, berries, apples, leafy greens, broccoli, sweet potatoes, olive oil, avocado.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Excluded:</strong> wheat bread and pasta, rice, oats, beans and lentils, peanuts (technically a
        legume), milk, cheese and yogurt, table sugar, chips, and packaged snacks.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">A Sample Day</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        <strong>Breakfast:</strong> scrambled eggs with spinach and avocado.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Lunch:</strong> grilled chicken salad with mixed greens, olive oil, walnuts, and berries.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Dinner:</strong> baked salmon, roasted sweet potato, steamed broccoli.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Snack:</strong> apple slices with almond butter.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Who It Might Suit</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        May suit people who tend to feel and perform better cutting out processed foods, added sugar, and
        refined carbs generally — regardless of whether the "ancestral" framing itself holds up. It may not
        suit people who rely on grains, legumes, or dairy as affordable staple protein, fiber, or calcium
        sources, since paleo-compliant meat, fish, nuts, and produce typically cost more; vegetarians and
        vegans, since the diet removes legumes and dairy, two of their main protein sources, without offering
        an alternative; or anyone with higher carbohydrate needs, including some athletes and certain medical
        contexts.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

